  "readTime": 1,
  "content": "<table align=\"center\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<div>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" style=\"padding-left: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p>#​621 — October 23, 2026</p></td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"padding-right: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191975/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">Read the Web Version</a></p></td>\n</tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0 12px;\"><p>Go Weekly</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191929/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/b5x3lqadlkbtjjepavzj.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191929/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.1em; line-height: 1.4em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"go.dev\">Interactive Refactoring in <code>gopls</code></a></span> — LSP refactorings can't 'ask' you anything, so <em>\"Add struct tags\"</em> in <code>gopls</code> just guessed <code>json</code> and camelCase. gopls can now pop up dialogs to collect your input. It uses a stateless, form-like protocol extension the Go and Dart teams have <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191930/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">proposed as an LSP standard</a>.</p>\n<p>Hongxiang Jiang (Go Team) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 For now it works in VS Code, with Neovim and Emacs support at proof-of-concept stage. A <em>Move declaration</em> refactoring is coming soon, with <em>Change signature</em> to follow.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191928/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\"><img height=\"110\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/c_limit,w_480,h_480,q_auto/copm/cac42246.png\" style=\"padding-top: 12px; padding-left: 12px; line-height: 100%;\" width=\"110\"/></a>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191928/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"master.dev\">Deploy Self-Healing Apps on Kubernetes</a></span> — Learn Kubernetes by building a real production setup. Erik Reinert takes you from a local Kind cluster to Amazon EKS with health probes, autoscaling, safe rollbacks, and GitOps with Argo CD.</p>\n<p>Master.dev <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #885 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><span>🚫</span> <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191931/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"github.com\">Go Hardcodes a Block on Broken eMudhra Certs</a></span> — Eight intermediate CA certificates had <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191932/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">trivially recoverable private keys</a>, so <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191933/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">Go 1.27.3 and 1.26.10</a> now refuse chains through them. It's hardcoded because, <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191934/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">as Filippo Valsorda notes</a>, Linux trust stores have no way to pass revocations on to apps.</p>\n<p>The Go Team </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>IN BRIEF:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>📺 The <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191935/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">GopherCon Europe 2026 talks</a> are now online, including <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191936/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">▶️ using Go to build for the Sega Dreamcast(!)</a> and a <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191937/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">▶️ panel with the Go team</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🇮🇳 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191938/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">GopherCon India 2026</a> takes place in Hyderabad on November 22, with tickets now on sale.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fiber has <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191939/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">marked its v2 module as deprecated</a>, so Go's tooling will now nudge v2 users toward Fiber v3.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>GO 1.28 &amp; PROPOSALS:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Go 1.28 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191940/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">will let you omit a composite literal's type</a> when the context supplies it (so <code>p = {1, 2}</code> will work).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><code>iter.Seq</code> could gain <code>Map</code> and <code>Filter</code> methods now that Go has generic methods, with <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191941/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">a new proposal's thread</a> already debating where to stop. <code>Fold</code>? <code>Keys</code>? <code>Map2</code>?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><code>net/http</code> is getting <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191942/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\"><code>Server.HandleRequest</code></a> (accepted last week), while <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191943/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\"><code>maps.Grow</code></a> and <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191944/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">an RSS-based <code>memory</code> profile</a> for <code>runtime/pprof</code> are both <em>likely accepts</em>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191945/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"go.dev\">Better HTTP/1 Connection Reuse</a></span> — You can now (mostly) stop draining HTTP/1 response bodies in Go 1.27 as the client now drains the leftovers in the background, within limits, making <code>io.Copy(io.Discard, resp.Body)</code> helpers largely redundant when you close the body.</p>\n<p>Nicholas Husin and Damien Neil (Go Team) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191946/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">The release notes</a> add a caveat the post skips: programs that let idle connections pile up (say, with <code>MaxIdleConns</code> set to 0 or a new <code>Client</code> per request) may get slower.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191947/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"internals-for-interns.com\">SIMD and Go: How the New Packages Work Under the Hood</a></span> — A heavily illustrated tour of Go's experimental SIMD support, covering what vector instructions actually do, the operations offered by <code>simd/archsimd</code> and the new portable <code>simd</code> package, and how the compiler turns them into machine code.</p>\n<p>Jesús Espino </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191948/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.tigerdata.com\">One Less System to Operate. Everything in SQL</a></span> — TimescaleDB extends Postgres so your metrics and app data share one database and one SQL join. <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191948/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">Get $1000 credit</a>.</p>\n<p>Tiger Data (creators of TimescaleDB) <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #885 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191949/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Spinning is a Bounded Bet: Why Go Hides Its Spinlocks</a> – Part one of a four-part series on spinning versus parking. <cite>Alex Rios</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191950/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">ConcurrentML: Improving on Go's Concurrency Model</a> – Makes the case for composable events as a fix for the limits of <code>select</code>. <cite>Nikhil Marathe</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191951/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Cutting a Go Git Server's RAM Usage by Two-Thirds</a> – Using <code>pprof</code> to take a push from 617MB to 199MB. <cite>Xe Iaso (Tigris)</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191952/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Why a Healthy Go Heap Can Still Get OOM-Killed in Kubernetes</a> <cite>Gulcan Topcu</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191953/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Dancing Backwards with Go: Why TDD Feels Weird</a> <cite>John Arundel</cite></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0;\"><p>🛠 Code &amp; Tools</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191954/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/g3awowau4bxptrdzhcni.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191954/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"mygo.egoist.dev\">MyGo: Build Desktop Apps in Go with No Cgo Required</a></span> — Build macOS, Windows and Linux apps with a UI drawn on the GPU from pure Go, or a web frontend in the system webview that calls Go via a generated TypeScript client. Apps can be just a few megabytes. <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191955/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">GitHub repo</a>.</p>\n<p>EGOIST </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191956/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"github.com\">TinyGo 0.43: Go for Microcontrollers and WebAssembly</a></span> — The Go compiler 'for small places' closes more gaps with regular Go, adding <code>testing/synctest</code>, parallel tests, panic recovery on Wasm, and more. Much of the work comes from Jake Bailey of TypeScript's Go port fame.</p>\n<p>The TinyGo Team </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 For some TinyGo fun, Hoani Bryson's <em>Trick Shot II</em> won this year's <em>Nokia 3310 Jam</em>, built on <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191957/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">his own Go engine</a> that even runs on a Pico inside a real 3310.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191958/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"github.com\">coregex: A Faster Drop-In Replacement for <code>regexp</code></a></span> — Brings the multi-engine design of Rust's <code>regex</code> crate to Go, with lazy DFA, PikeVM, one-pass matching and SIMD prefilters. The author claims 15–240x speedups.</p>\n<p>Andrey Kolkov </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 The standard library may close some of that gap: <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191959/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">a port of RE2's lazy DFA to <code>regexp</code></a> is up for review, with early benchmarks showing big performance improvements.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191960/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">gopls 0.24</a> – The official Go language server moves to LSP 3.18 and adds hover, highlighting and references in Go assembly files <em>(interactive refactoring is covered in the feature at the top of the issue)</em>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191961/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">GoReleaser 2.19</a> – The release automation tool graduates several experimental features and adds tunable zstd compression for archives.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191962/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">fasthttp 1.76</a> – The fast HTTP package gets extra hardening against request smuggling and message-framing attacks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191963/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">decimal 1.6</a> – Arbitrary-precision fixed-point decimals, now with <code>Sqrt</code>, <code>Root</code> and BSON Decimal128.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191964/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">chi 5.5</a> – Lightweight router, now with <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191965/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">RFC 9745 deprecation middleware</a>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px;\">\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📰 Classifieds</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>Build your Golang app faster. <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191966/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">WarpBuild</a> runners are twice as fast at half the cost. <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191966/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Get $50 in free credits</a> with code <code>START50</code>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191967/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">CodeAF</a>, an open-source coding harness written in Go: #1 of ten on DeepSWE on the same model, at 2x to 25x less per fix than the others.</p>\n<p>🪝 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191968/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Send webhooks from your Go service in minutes</a>. Svix handles retries, signing, delivery logs, and a portal for your customers.</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0;\"><p>⭐ And finally…</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191969/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/xtdpxgsdvsakrnew3e6m.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191969/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"go.dev\">Help Set Go's Priorities with the 2026 Go Developer Survey</a></span> — The Go team uses the results to decide what to work on. <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191970/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">Last year</a>, the top frustration was keeping Go code idiomatic and following best practices, which led to the <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/191971/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">rewritten <code>go fix</code></a> in Go 1.26 and more modernizers in 1.27.</p>\n<p>Cameron Balahan (Go Team) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n</td></tr>\n</table>\n",
  "attachments": []
}